{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790527377,"nonce":"zVlrt+9UrNX0o+qH1CYChiU4buw=","title":"caribou serve: a dev server for a project in the browser","message":"caribou serve [--port N] [--lan] [\u003cproject.cbproj\u003e] builds the project for wasm32-wasip1-threads into target/, writes Ash's page beside the module, and serves target/ over HTTP with COOP/COEP/CORP and no-store. localhost is a secure origin, so no certificate is needed; --lan also binds the LAN address, which a browser must be told to treat as secure. It watches the project file, its class paths and its plugin crates, rebuilds on change, and reloads open pages through an EventSource the served index.html listens on. A failed build is reported and the last good one stays served. The page and browser host come from Ash as a library (requested from ash-b7: today write_page lives in ash_cli and the host is found beside the ash binary). Background: a self-signed HTTPS test server broke Chrome's nested module workers after the certificate click-through, which is why plain HTTP on localhost is the default.","files":null}]}